<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-2">2 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">India institutes the Bharat Ratna, its highest civilian award</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-prasad">Rajendra Prasad</a> established the Bharat Ratna to honour exceptional national service. The first recipients were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-rajagopalachari">C. Rajagopalachari</a>, Sarvepalli Radhakrishnan and the physicist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-v-raman">C. V. Raman</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-10">10 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">BOAC Comet airliner breaks up over the Mediterranean</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A de Havilland Comet, the world's first jet airliner, disintegrated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a>, killing all 35 aboard. Investigations traced the disaster to metal fatigue, transforming aircraft design and testing.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-14">14 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Marilyn Monroe marries Joe DiMaggio</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The film star and the retired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Yankees hero married at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> City Hall. The marriage lasted less than a year, though DiMaggio remained devoted to Monroe until her death.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-21">21 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">USS Nautilus, the first nuclear-powered submarine, is launched</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">First Lady Mamie <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> christened Nautilus at Groton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>. Able to stay submerged for weeks, it sailed under the North Pole in 1958 and transformed undersea warfare.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-1">1 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Castle Bravo, the largest US nuclear test, contaminates the Pacific</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The hydrogen bomb tested at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bikini-atoll">Bikini Atoll</a> yielded 15 megatons, far more than predicted. Fallout sickened Marshall Islanders and the crew of the Japanese fishing boat Lucky Dragon No. 5.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/oxford" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oxford.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-6">6 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Roger Bannister runs the first sub-four-minute mile</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">On a windy evening at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>'s Iffley Road track, the medical student Roger Bannister ran the mile in 3 minutes 59.4 seconds, helped by pacemakers Chris Brasher and Chris Chataway.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-17">17 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Supreme Court strikes down school segregation</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brown-v-board-of-education">Brown v. Board of Education</a> the US Supreme Court ruled unanimously that racially segregated public schools were unconstitutional, overturning the 'separate but equal' doctrine in education.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Battle of Dien Bien Phu</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Dien Bien Phu was fought in 1954 in northern Vietnam between the French army and the Viet Minh under General Vo Nguyen Giap.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/geneva-accords-1954" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geneva-accords-1954.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/geneva-accords-1954">Geneva Accords (1954)</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Geneva Accords were the agreements concluded at the Geneva Conference in July 1954 that ended the First Indochina War.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/1954-guatemalan-coup-detat" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1954-guatemalan-coup-detat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/1954-guatemalan-coup-detat">1954 Guatemalan coup d'état</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The 1954 Guatemalan coup d'état overthrew the elected president Jacobo Árbenz, whose land reform had expropriated unused land of the United Fruit Company.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/brown-v-board-of-education" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brown-v-board-of-education.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/brown-v-board-of-education">Brown v. Board of Education</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Brown v. Board of Education was a unanimous ruling of the United States Supreme Court on 17 May 1954 that state laws establishing racial segregation in public schools violated the Fourteenth Amendment's Equal Protection Clause.</p></div></li></ol></section>
